oater】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests】\nL122:   * 【65†Actions】\nL123:   * 【66†Projects】\nL124:   * 【67†Security and quality】\nL125:   * 【68†Insights】\nL126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: 【69†Image: xuezhantong†avatars.githubusercontent.com】\nL130: \nL131: 【62†zk-debloater】\nL132: \nL133: Public\nL134: \nL135:   * 【70†Notifications】You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL136:   * 【70†Fork0 (0)】\nL137:   * 【70†Star0 (0)】You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: \nL139: ## About\nL140: \nL141: No description, website, or topics provided.\nL142: ### Resources\nL143: \nL144: 【71†Readme】\nL145: \nL146: 【72†Activity】\nL147: \nL148: ### Stars\nL149: \nL150: 0 stars\nL151: \nL152: ### Watchers\nL153: \nL154: 0 watching\nL155: \nL156: ### Forks\nL157: \nL158: 【73†0 forks】\nL159: \nL160: 【74†Report repository】\nL161: \nL162: main\nL163: \nL164: 【75†Branches】【76†Tags】\nL165: \nL166: [Input: Go to file]\nL167: \nL168: Go to file\nL169: \nL170: Code\nL171: \nL172: Open more actions menu\nL173: \nL174: ## Latest commit\nL175: \nL176: \nL177: \nL178: ## History\nL179: \nL180: 3 Commits\nL181: \nL182: ## Folders and files\nL183: \nL184: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL185: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL186: 【77†README.md】  | 【77†README.md】  |    |\nL187: \nL188: ## Repository files navigation\nL189: \nL190:   *   * 【78†README】\nL191: \nL192: More items\nL193: # Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks\nL194: \nL195: This artifact accompanies Sound Debloating of Redundant Checks in Zero-Knowledge Machine-Learning Circuits. The debloater removes redundant checks using abstract interpretation and a provenance graph. It accepts Circom sources or PLONKish circuit JSON. The proof builders measure proving time, verification time, and proof size before and after debloating.\nL196: \nL197: Note: the files are not shared on Github. Access it through 【79†figshare†doi.org】.\nL198: See the 【80†anonymized paper】, especially Sections 5–6, for the method and experiments.\nL199: ## Requirements and quick start\nL200: \nL201: Run commands from this directory. The core workflow uses Python 3.10+, Rust/Cargo 1.93.1, a C/C++ compiler, and GNU Make. Cargo needs network access on a fresh machine; `Cargo.lock` pins Rust dependencies.\nL202: The paper's timing experiments used Ubuntu 24 LTS on an AMD EPYC 9754 with 768 GiB RAM. The small example below takes seconds after compilation. Full experiments require substantially more memory and can take days. The paper's machine configuration is a reference environment, not a measured minimum.\nL203: \nL204:     # Check file integrity before builds create additional files.\nL205:     python3 scripts/artifact.py check\nL206: \nL207:     cargo build --locked --release -p zk-debloater -p halo2-builder -p iterative-verify\nL208:     # Generate a small MLP, prove/verify, debloat, then prove/verify again.\nL209:     python3 scripts/artifact.py smoke --out runs/smoke\nL210: The example reduces 47 gates and 75 lookups to 44 gates and 44 lookups. Both proofs should verify, with proof sizes of 35,584 and 26,592 bytes. The command fails if either proof stage fails or the lookup count is not reduced. Results are written to `runs/smoke/smoke.json`. This small example demonstrates the workflow; it is separate from the paper's benchmark measurements.\nL211: ## Recompute tables and the scaling figure\nL212: \nL213:     python3 -m venv .venv\nL214:     . .venv/bin/activate\nL215:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL216:     python3 scripts/artifact.py report --out runs/report\nL217:     python3 scripts/validate-inputs.py\nL218: \nL219: The report command analyzes the supplied measurements and writes:\nL220:   * `rq1.tex`: Table 2, covering constraint reduction and proof-system costs.\nL221:   * `fig-scaling.pdf`: Figure 3, covering the scaling experiments.\nL222:   * `rq3-partial.tex`: the O2, Distilling, CirC, No-Graph, and Ours columns of Table 3. The No-Graph LSTM reduction is marked `unverified` because independent compiled before/after counts are not available in its saved results.\nL223: Clap and korrekt are evaluated separately using the commands below. `validate-inputs.py` checks the six zkml named-model constraint counts, the Circom include paths, and the five SMT encoded constraint counts without running long proof or SMT queries.\nL224: Experiment  | Inputs and measurements\nL225: --- | ---\nL226: RQ1 / Table 2  | `bench_results/named/san`: 13 debloater summaries; `bench_results/named/pv`: baseline/debloated proof measurements\nL227: RQ2 / Figure 3  | `bench_results/scaling`: 13 curves, each with parameter multipliers 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32\nL228: RQ3 / Table 3  | `r1cs-inputs/comparison`: Circom and ZoKrates sources, compiler driver, and five result JSONs\nL229: No-Graph  | `bench_results/named/one_at_a_time/results.json` and `iterative-verify`\nL230: Clap  | `plonkish-inputs/clap-comparison`\nL231: korrekt structural checks  | `halo2-analyzer-comparison`\nL232: Exact SMT comparison  | `smt-comparison/results_scaled` and the five `mini_*.json` inputs under `plonkish-inputs/zkml/circuits`\nL233: Mechanized proofs  | `mechanized-proof/kernel` and `src/coq_emitter`\nL234: RQ1 covers MLP, LeNet, RNN, GRU, LSTM, and BERT for both zkml and ezkl, plus MobileNet for ezkl. The number of successful proof repetitions varies by model; consult the individual PV JSON files when aggregating measurements.\nL235: ## Run new experiments\nL236: \nL237: Write new measurements under `runs/` so that the supplied reference measurements remain available for comparison. Drivers generate intermediate circuits under `plonkish-inputs/*/circuits`. Use fresh output directories for independent runs; the named-benchmark driver resumes cached results.\nL238: Some experiment drivers record failures or skipped runs without returning a nonzero process exit code. Check that every requested baseline and debloated record has `exit_code == 0` and contains proof and verification metrics.\nL239: ### zkml named models and scaling\nL240: The generators directly construct PLONKish circuits modeling zkml gadgets. They use synthetic inputs and weights; pretrained checkpoints and external datasets are not required. The reference zkml checkout is `43789582671f16148dd02cbac5654f462d4fe3e4` in 【81†ddkang/zkml】.\nL241: \nL242:     mkdir -p runs/named\nL243:     export RAYON_NUM_THREADS=1\nL244:     export RUST_MIN_STACK=268435456\nL245: \nL246:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase zkml_gen --benches bench_ptb_rnn\nL247:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase debloat --pipeline zkml \\\nL248:       --benches bench_ptb_rnn --out-dir runs/named --timeout 14400\nL249:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase prove_verify --pipeline zkml \\\nL250:       --benches bench_ptb_rnn --in-dir runs/named --out-json runs/zkml-rnn-pv.json \\\nL251:       --reps 3 --timeout 14400\nL252: \nL253:     python3 plonkish-inputs/zkml/scripts/scaling_study.py \\\nL254:       --models rnn --multipliers 1 --output runs/zkml-rnn-scaling.json\nL255: `--benches` accepts a comma-separated list or `all`. Named models are `bench_mnist_mlp`, `bench_lenet5`, `bench_ptb_rnn`, `bench_ptb_gru`, `bench_ptb_lstm`, and `bench_bert_tiny`. The recurrent generator configurations are RNN: hidden dimension 50, 35 timesteps; GRU: 45, 25; and LSTM: 40, 20.\nL256: \nL257: For the complete scaling sweep, omit `--models` and `--multipliers`. The six families are `fc_mlp`, `lenet_cnn`, `rnn`, `gru`, `lstm`, and `transformer_encoder`.\nL258: ### ezkl named models and scaling\nL259: The ezkl workflow additionally needs Git, Rustup, PyTorch, ONNX, NumPy, and ezkl's native build dependencies. `scripts/setup-ezkl.sh` fetches ezkl checkout `e196b111c1bafaa61b92ae431cd3c3fe9371da05`, installs its specified Rust toolchain, and builds the extractor using ezkl's lockfile. Dependency revisions and observed Python environment versions are recorded in `PROVENANCE.json`.\nL260: \nL261:     bash scripts/setup-ezkl.sh\nL262: \nL263:     # In an environment with torch, onnx, and numpy installed:\nL264:     python3 plonkish-inputs/ezkl/scripts/generate_model.py bench_mnist_mlp\nL265:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase ezkl_extract \\\nL266:       --benches bench_mnist_mlp --timeout 14400\nL267:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase debloat --pipeline ezkl \\\nL268:       --benches bench_mnist_mlp --out-dir runs/named --timeout 14400\nL269:     python3 plonkish-inputs/bench/bench_driver.py --phase prove_verify --pipeline ezkl \\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - THU-HAS/MegaZK · GitHub (https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK\",\"lineno\":220}); Total lines: 383\nL58:     * EXPLORE BY TOPIC\nL59:       * 【34†AI】\nL60:       * 【35†Software Development】\nL71:     * SUPPORT & SERVICES\nL72:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL73:       * 【44†Customer support†support.github.com】\nL74:       * 【45†Community forum】\nL75:       * 【46†Trust center】\nL76:       * 【47†Partners】\nL77: \nL78: 【48†View all resources】\nL79: \nL80:   * Open Source\nL81: \nL82:     * COMMUNITY\nL83:       * 【49†GitHub SponsorsFund open source developers】\nL84:     * PROGRAMS\nL85:       * 【50†Security Lab†securitylab.github.com】\nL86:       * 【51†Maintainer Community†maintainers.github.com】\nL87:       * 【52†GitHub Stars†stars.github.com】\nL88:       * 【53†Archive Program†archiveprogram.github.com】\nL89: \nL90:     * REPOSITORIES\nL91:       * 【54†Topics】\nL92:       * 【55†Trending】\nL93:       * 【56†Collections】\nL94: \nL95:   * Enterprise\nL96: \nL97:     * ENTERPRISE SOLUTIONS\nL98:       * 【19†Enterprise platformAI-powered developer platf